# 40 — Security: auth and authorization

## Purpose
Keep every endpoint protected exactly the way its module already protects endpoints, and keep the
permission registry consistent with the frontend's permission gating.

## When it applies
Adding/changing endpoints, permission checks, roles/permissions, auth/user code or broadcast channels.

## Existing project convention
- Auth: Sanctum Bearer tokens (7-day `expiration`); `/login` rate limited in `AuthController::login()`. `EnsureUserIsActive` (alias `active`) deletes the
  current token and returns 403 for inactive users. `UserController::deactivateUser` deletes all tokens.
- Spatie guard is always `web`. No Policies, Gates, `$this->authorize()` or route `permission:` middleware.
- Authorization is **imperative, at the top of the action method**, in one of three styles. The style
  is per controller — and `RoomParController` mixes two:

| Style | Used by | Admin bypass |
|---|---|---|
| `AuthorizesSpatiePermissions::authorizePermissions([...anyOf])` + domain `allow*()` wrappers | `BookingController`, `RoomController`, `RoomTypeController`, `RoomStatusBlockController`, `Housekeeping*Controller`, `LaundryRequestController`, `RoomCleaningReleaseController` | **none** |
| private `checkPermission(string)` copied per controller | `PosController`, `DayClosingController`, F&B and inventory masters, settings, users/roles, `RoomParController` (12 of its 13 checks) | `Admin` (some copies also `Super Admin`) |
| `App\Services\InventoryAuthorization::assert*()` (static) | inventory, GRN, procurement, accounting | `Admin` + `Super Admin` |

- Permission names are kebab-case `{module}-{action}` (`reservation-edit`, `housekeeping-assignable`,
  `pos-settle`); legacy coarse names (`reservation`, `manage-rooms`, `view-rooms`) are still accepted
  in any-of lists.
- **Permission registry — two coexisting paths:**
  - Canonical list `Database\Seeders\RolePermissionSeeder::permissionNames()`. `RoleController::permissions()`,
    `store()` and `update()` call `ensureCanonicalPermissionsExist()`, which `firstOrCreate`s every name
    (guard `web`) but grants nothing. HK and reservation permissions (`housekeeping-assignable`,
    `housekeeping-cleaning-availability`, `reservation-hold-room`) exist only this way.
  - Permission migrations, used for POS permissions that must exist **and be granted to roles** on
    deploy: `add_pos_day_closing_unlock_permission`, `add_pos_business_date_override_permission`,
    `add_pos_kds_force_clear_permission`, `add_pos_mini_dash_permission`.
- Row scope:
  - F&B outlet access via `PosController::userCanAccessRestaurant()` → `authorizeOrderAccess()` /
    `authorizeRestaurantId()` (403 `'You do not have access to this outlet.'`); duplicated in
    `DayClosingController`, `routes/channels.php`, `PropertyFinancialSummaryService`.
  - HK room stock: `HousekeepingRoomStockController::requireHkDepartmentForUser()` requires membership
    of department `HKP` (Admin exempt) → 403 `'Only Housekeeping department users can manage room stock.'`
  - Bookings, rooms and other HK endpoints have no row scope.
- HK assignment needs `housekeeping-assignable` (cleaning) or `housekeeping-checkout-inspection-assign`
  (pending checkout inspection), and the assignee must be an active user in an active `is_housekeeping` department
  (`assertCanAssignHousekeepingStaff()`, `assertCanAssignCheckoutInspection()`, `housekeepingAssignableStaff()`).
- Guest ID images are stored on the private disk `config('guest_identity.disk')` (default `local`) and returned
  as paths plus signed `guest_identity_urls` in booking responses (see 30-api-contract).

## Required behavior
- New action method: call the **same helper the surrounding methods of that controller use**, at the top.
  In `BookingController`, add/reuse an `allowReservation*()` wrapper; in HK controllers use
  `allowHousekeepingOperate([self::HK_...])` / `allowHousekeepingViewSection()`; in `RoomParController`
  use its `checkPermission` like its neighbours.
- Copy the exact `checkPermission` variant of the controller you are in (null-user handling and
  Super Admin bypass differ between copies).
- New permission: always add it to `RolePermissionSeeder::permissionNames()`. Also ship a migration like
  `2026_09_22_085400_add_pos_mini_dash_permission.php` (`firstOrCreate` with `guard_name => 'web'`,
  grant to named roles if present, `forgetCachedPermissions()`, `down()` deletes it) when existing roles
  must receive it on deploy — follow what sibling permissions of the same module do, and ask if unclear.
- POS/day-closing endpoints taking an order/outlet must call `authorizeOrderAccess()` / `authorizeRestaurantId()` like siblings do.
- HK room-stock endpoints keep `requireHkDepartmentForUser()`.
- Only an `Admin` may assign/modify the `Admin` role; keep the last-active-admin guard in `UserController`.

## Things Cursor must NOT do
- Do not add an Admin bypass to `AuthorizesSpatiePermissions` or remove it from `checkPermission`.
- Do not "unify" `RoomParController` onto one helper style as a side change.
- Do not introduce Policies/Gates or route middleware for permissions.
- Do not remove legacy permission names from any-of lists (existing roles rely on them).
- Do not use the `sanctum` guard for roles/permissions.
- Do not change how guest ID images are stored or exposed as a side change; raise it as a question.

## Important examples from the existing codebase
- `BookingController::allowReservationCreateSingle()` vs `allowReservationCreateGroup()` (decided from raw input before validation in `store()`).
- `PosController::checkPermission()` (401 when no user; Admin + Super Admin bypass) vs `DepartmentController::checkPermission()` (Admin bypass; passes when user is null) vs `RoomParController::checkPermission()` (Admin bypass; passes when user is null).
- `RoomStatusBlockController::authorizeStatusBlockStore()` — permission depends on block status.
- `RoleController::ensureCanonicalPermissionsExist()` — runtime registry sync from the seeder list.
